# Git

A question is not permission. `reset`, `rebase`, `push --force`, `push --force-with-lease`, `branch -D`, `clean`, `checkout -- <path>`, and `worktree remove --force` run only on an order that names the operation and the target SHA or ref. That holds for the agent's own uncommitted edit too: undo it with an edit. Before removing any worktree, run `git -C <worktree> status --short`; undo what it lists, then remove it without `--force`, from outside the worktree.

This holds for a conflict that another PR's merge caused: ask how to solve it (rebase and force-push, or merge `main` into the branch) before running either.

When asked about such an operation, answer with the current `HEAD` SHA, the target SHA, what would disappear, and the exact command. Then wait.

- Finished work goes to a PR in the same turn, without waiting for an ask: commit on the Issue's branch, push it, and open the PR (`.cursor/rules/workflow.mdc`). The owner reviews on the PR; uncommitted changes on this machine cannot be reviewed. Do not stop at "may I commit?".
- Long work runs in an agent worktree (`git worktree add .dev/<branch> <branch>`), not in the main working tree: the owner may switch that tree to another branch to review.
- This covers the Issue's own branch only. Never commit to `main` or push to it, and never merge.
- `git branch --show-current` is part of the command that commits. A chain that creates a branch can fail before it does, and the commit then lands on `main`.
- A push that follows a test is chained to it so that a failed test stops the push (`./build.ps1; if ($LASTEXITCODE -eq 0) { git push }`).
- Read `gh pr view <n> --json state` before pushing to a branch that has a PR. A push to the branch of a merged PR recreates the deleted branch and reaches nothing.
- If history was rewritten, put the SHA from before the rewrite on the first line of the reply.
- Never skip hooks (`--no-verify`) or signing unless the owner orders it.
- Never commit key material, a real license token, or a secret, test fixtures included (`.cursor/rules/security.mdc`). Read `git status --short` and the staged file list before each commit.
